{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-twoM-MDM",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Fig. 19 caption, p. 433 (Sec. VII): air (LH)^10 1.828L M D M 1.828L (HL)^8 quartz; Figs. 18-20",
  "Notes": "Two-M (metal-dielectric-metal) UV bandpass filter at 2536 A: Al 250 A / cryolite spacer (QWOT at 3900 A) / Al 250 A, matched and antireflected with cryolite / n=1.55 quarter-wave stacks\nSpecification: MDM absorbing stack with spacer optimised for Psi_max (0.546 at D = QWOT at 3900 A), then admittance-matched and antireflected at 2536 A.\nReported: p. 432-433: Psi_max of the MDM absorbing stack is optimum, 0.546, when the spacer D is a QWOT at 3900 A (Fig. 18: Psi_max vs spacer QWOT 0.2-0.5 um peaks at 0.39 um); (0.739)^2 = 0.546. Fig. 19 (linear T, 0.2-0.4 um; dashed = nondispersive n = 0.2 - j2.8): peak T ~0.54 just above 0.25 um; long-wave leak ~0.13-0.15 near 0.29 um. Fig. 20: same on a log scale, ~1e-3 below 0.24 um. Check computed here (nondispersive): T = 0.381, R = 0.206 at 253.6 nm; peak T = 0.549 at 255.6 nm with R ~0; T = 0.149 at 290 nm, 0.160 at 295 nm, ~9e-4 at 220-240 nm.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). Per the Fig. 19 caption: L = QWOT at 2536 A, index 1.36 (cryolite); D = QWOT at 3900 A, index 1.36 (= 390/253.6 QWOT at 253.6 nm); H = QWOT at 2536 A, index 1.55 (the 1.55 material, i.e. thorium oxyfluoride B of Table VI, not MgO); M = Al 250 A, n = 0.2 - j2.8; quartz 1.47. Expanded: (LH)^10 (20), 1.828L, M, D, M, 1.828L, (HL)^8 (16): 41 layers. The paper's solid curve uses Hunter's dispersive Al data (J. Phys. 25, 154 (1964)), not printed and not reproduced here. The computed peak (0.549 near 255.6 nm) agrees with the dashed curve of Fig. 19, though T at exactly 253.6 nm is lower.\nLayers from Fig. 19 caption, p. 433 (Sec. VII): air (LH)^10 1.828L M D M 1.828L (HL)^8 quartz; Figs. 18-20; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours peak T 0.549 at 255.6 nm; T 0.15 at 290 nm; paper 0.546 (the absorbing stack’s limit), ~0.54 plotted just above 250 nm; leak ~0.15 near 290 nm.",
